      unitsNote: "2,101 sub-standard plus 122 standard across the three project areas. Add the highway estimates and documented removals approach 5,500 dwellings.",
      familiesNote: "1,139 of 1,242 families (91.7 per cent) were recorded as non-white, in a city that was roughly 17 per cent Black in 1940.",
      businessesNote: "236 businesses and six institutions, counted in the two projects that recorded them. Ybor City's business losses were never tallied.",
      note: "The counterweight the project's editorial guardrail asks for. Through the June 1974 Urban Renewal Directory Tampa had $36,814,442 in federal urban renewal grants approved and $28,242,421 disbursed: Maryland Avenue $3,668,888 approved and disbursed; Riverfront $8,743,015 / $8,179,278; Ybor City $7,828,591 / $5,754,903; Code Enforcement $4,249,488 / $4,249,488; Interim Assistance $102,048 / $102,048; Neighborhood Development Program $12,222,412 / $6,287,816. Set that beside Water Street's $3B of private investment sixty years later.",
